  reel_script says the video showed a mismatch with police accusations and that there was no basis for the attack allegation; the article says the video showed inconsistency and the court found the allegation not believable, but the reel phrasing 'வீடியோவில் தெரிந்தது' loosely overstates it as settled fact and drops the 'not believable' wording (minor).
  reel_script and caption say the case was 'quashed' over 'questioning' — the article's key point that the police officer's phone was snatched and the case was allegedly false (பொய் வழக்கு) is omitted from the reel_script.
  caption states the case was against 3 people who 'challenged' the case, but the article says the petition was filed by the three and the court's quashing was for a case of attempting to assault police; the caption's 'மாணவர் ... வீடியோவில் ... முரண்பாடு இருப்பதாக நீதிமன்றத்தில் காட்டப்பட்டது' merges the video point with the allegation in one sentence, mixing two facts.
  reel_script omits that the lawyer Asan Mohamed Jinnah argued the case and that lawyer Sarita raised the police station issue, an important party-level fact of who did what.
  slides_spec 'வழக்கு ஏன் பொய்?' slide says the case was 'காவல் அதிகாரியைத் தாக்க முயன்றதாக வழக்கு' but the article's reason also includes preventing officers from doing duty, which is omitted, and the line 'கேள்வி கேட்பது குற்றமல்ல என வாதம்' attributes an argument, not a finding, as a conclusion of the judgment.
